{"slug": "bbq-chicken-and-mushroom-tostadas", "nyt_id": 12334, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/12334-bbq-chicken-and-mushroom-tostadas", "name": "BBQ Chicken and Mushroom Tostadas", "author": "Martha Rose Shulman", "description": "This recipe offers a novel way to create chips. When corn tortillas are toasted in the oven, they get hard, not crispy. When they are toasted in the microwave, they get that crispy snap. This method requires a little patience: Zap a tortilla for a minute and it will be soggy, even wet, on the bottom. Flip it over and zap it again, and all the moisture will be gone. Sometimes it takes a few turns, as every microwave is different. Watch closely, though, as they tortillas burn quickly once dry.", "servings": {"text": "4 servings.", "min": 4, "max": 4, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 45, "raw": {"total": "45 minutes"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 whole chicken breast on the bone, skinned and split, or 2 boneless, skinless chicken breasts, 1 ⅓ to 1 ½ pounds", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "whole chicken breast on the bone", "preparation": "skinned and split, or 2 boneless, skinless chicken breasts, 1 ⅓ to 1 ½ pounds", "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "2 ½ quarts water", "group": null, "quantity": 2.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "quart", "item": "water", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 onion, quartered", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "onion", "preparation": "quartered", "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "2 garlic cloves, peeled and crushed", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "garlic cloves", "preparation": "peeled and crushed", "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "½ teaspoon dried thyme or oregano, or a combination", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tsp", "item": "dried thyme or oregano", "preparation": "or a combination", "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "Salt to taste", "group": null, "quantity": null, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt to taste", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "1 tablespoon extra virgin olive oil", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "extra virgin olive oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "8 white or cremini mushrooms, trimmed and sliced", "group": null, "quantity": 8.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "white or cremini mushrooms", "preparation": "trimmed and sliced", "optional": false}, {"position": 9, "raw": "¼ cup prepared barbecue sauce", "group": null, "quantity": 0.25, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "prepared barbecue sauce", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 10, "raw": "6 corn tortillas, halved and toasted in the microwave (see above)", "group": null, "quantity": 6.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "corn tortillas", "preparation": "halved and toasted in the microwave (see above)", "optional": false}, {"position": 11, "raw": "2 cups arugula", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "arugula", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 12, "raw": "1 to 2 tablespoons fresh lime juice", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": 2.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "fresh lime juice", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 13, "raw": "6 radishes", "group": null, "quantity": 6.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "radishes", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Cut the tortillas in half or into quarters, or leave whole. Place as many as will fit in one layer on a plate and microwave on full power for 1 minute. Turn over the tortilla pieces (they’ll be wet on the bottom) and microwave again for a minute. The chips should be browned and crisp. If they are not, turn over once more and microwave for another 30 seconds to a minute. Repeat with the remaining tortillas until all of them are done. They’ll remain crispy for several hours. Note: The process will take less time if you air-dry the tortillas on a rack for an hour or so before microwaving."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Combine the water, the quartered onion and the whole crushed garlic cloves in a 2-quart saucepan and bring to a simmer over medium heat. Add the chicken breasts and bring back to a simmer. Skim off any foam that rises, then add the dried herbs. Partly cover, reduce the heat to low and simmer 15 to 20 minutes, until the chicken is cooked through (cut one in half to check; the meat should not be pink). Add salt to taste. Allow the chicken to cool in the broth if there is time. Remove the chicken from the broth when it is cool enough to handle. Remove from the bone and shred by pulling strips of chicken off the top of the breast, with the grain. You should have about 4 cups of shredded chicken. Strain the chicken broth and refrigerate overnight (the next morning, skim off and discard the fat and freeze the broth in smaller containers)."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Heat the olive oil over medium-high heat in a large, heavy skillet and add the mushrooms. Cook, stirring, until they are seared and beginning to soften, and add salt and pepper to taste. Continue cooking until mushrooms are just tender, a couple of minutes, and remove from the heat. Add the chicken and the barbecue sauce and stir until the meat and mushrooms are coated lightly with the sauce."}, {"position": 4, "text": "Toast the tortillas in the microwave. Toss the arugula with lime juice. Place a spoonful of the chicken and mushroom mixture on the tortillas, top with a handful of arugula, garnish with radishes and serve."}], "nutrition": {"calories": 242.5, "protein_g": 18.6, "carbohydrates_g": 28.2, "fat_g": 6.4, "saturated_fat_g": 1.1, "trans_fat_g": 0.0, "cholesterol_mg": 49.6, "sodium_mg": 1820.7, "fiber_g": 3.4, "sugar_g": 8.1, "unsaturated_fat_g": 4.4, "source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": ["dairy-free", "gluten-free", "healthy"], "course": ["main course"], "meal": ["dinner"], "occasion": [], "other": ["barbecue sauce", "braise", "chicken breast", "cremini", "microwave", "saute", "tostada", "white mushroom", "winter"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2012/03/13/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-facebookJumbo-v2.jpg", "alt": "Barbecued Chicken and Mushroom Tostadas", "primary": true}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 23}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2012-03-12", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T23:55:35.884733+00:00", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "bbq-pork", "nyt_id": 5643, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/5643-bbq-pork", "name": "BBQ Pork", "author": "Jonathan Reynolds", "description": "", "servings": {"text": "6 servings", "min": 6, "max": 6, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 210, "raw": {"total": "About 3 hours 30 minutes"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 6-to-7 ½-pound Boston-butt portion of the pork shoulder", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "6-to-7 ½-pound boston-butt portion of the pork shoulder", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "2 tablespoons mustard", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "mustard", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "2 tablespoons cumin", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "cumin", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "2 tablespoons paprika dolce", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "paprika dolce", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "1 tablespoon salt", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "⅔ teaspoon cayenne pepper", "group": null, "quantity": 0.6666666666666666, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tsp", "item": "cayenne pepper", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "2 tablespoons sugar", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "sugar", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Smear the meat with the mustard."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Mix the other ingredients and press evenly into the meat."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Water-smoke for 3 to 4 hours. When the temperature reads 170, take the meat out."}], "nutrition": {"calories": 1238.1, "protein_g": 88.7, "carbohydrates_g": 6.7, "fat_g": 92.8, "saturated_fat_g": 31.9, "trans_fat_g": 0.0, "cholesterol_mg": 362.3, "sodium_mg": 1301.7, "fiber_g": 1.3, "sugar_g": 4.5, "unsaturated_fat_g": 51.4, "source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": [], "course": ["main course"], "meal": ["dinner"], "occasion": [], "other": ["barbecues", "mustard", "pork shoulder", "roasts", "smoke"]}, "images": [], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 10}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2000-07-09", "scraped_at": "2026-09-07T07:44:30.203840+00:00", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "bbq-porkette-with-fried-potatoes-and-scallion-hash", "nyt_id": 11213, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/11213-bbq-porkette-with-fried-potatoes-and-scallion-hash", "name": "BBQ Porkette With Fried Potatoes and Scallion Hash", "author": "Sam Sifton", "description": "Porkette is shoulder meat injected with brine, inserted into netting and \"smoked\" with burned-hickory mist. It is an industrial food product (and so's your hanger steak, pal), but it's a more than decent one. And at $4.99 a pound, dinner for four costs less than $20. But it also makes an excellent breakfast. At the Hope & Anchor restaurant in Red Hook, Brooklyn, Dianna Munz served a barbecued ham and scallion hash with two fried eggs, but sometimes hash is better consumed at home. Made with Porkette, Munz's dish takes on a slightly gruffer flavor that is matched perfectly by the sweet of the barbecue sauce and the thick run of an easy-cooked egg yolk. Members of the smart set will cut their potatoes thin the night before. This makes the final preparation of the dish on a weekend morning a snap. Just add hot coffee. Sam Sifton", "servings": {"text": "Serves 2 as a main course", "min": 2, "max": 2, "yield_type": "pieces"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 45, "raw": {"total": "45 minutes"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 tablespoon butter", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "butter", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "½ cup finely chopped onion", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "finely chopped onion", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 teaspoon finely chopped garlic", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tsp", "item": "finely chopped garlic", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "½ cup ketchup", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "ketchup", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "3 tablespoons brown sugar", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "brown sugar", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "3 tablespoons cider vinegar", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "cider vinegar", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "1 tablespoon spicy brown mustard", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "spicy brown mustard", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "1 tablespoon Worcestershire sauce", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "worcestershire sauce", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 9, "raw": "Salt and freshly ground pepper", "group": null, "quantity": null, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt and freshly ground pepper", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 10, "raw": "About 2 quarts peanut oil", "group": null, "quantity": null, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "about 2 quarts peanut oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 11, "raw": "4 medium Idaho potatoes", "group": null, "quantity": 4.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "medium idaho potatoes", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 12, "raw": "Salt", "group": null, "quantity": null, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 13, "raw": "1 tablespoon olive oil", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "olive oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 14, "raw": "1 tablespoon butter", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "butter", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 15, "raw": "½ cup diced Porkette", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "diced porkette", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 16, "raw": "½ cup chopped scallion", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "chopped scallion", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 17, "raw": "2 tablespoons chopped cilantro", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "chopped cilantro", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Make the sauce: in a saucepan, melt the butter over low heat; add the onions and garlic and cook until the onions are soft but not brown. Add the remaining ingredients and simmer until slightly thickened, about 20 minutes. Let cool."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Make the potatoes: fill a deep pot with 2 inches of oil. Heat the oil to 320 degrees. Meanwhile, peel and julienne the potatoes and drop them into a bowl of water. Run water into the bowl until it runs clear. Dry well. Fry in batches so the potatoes are blanched but not browned, about 1 ½ minutes. Drain. Increase the oil temperature to 375 degrees and fry a second time in batches until golden brown. Toss them in a paper-towel-lined bowl and season with salt."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Make the hash: heat the oil and the butter in a large pan over medium-high heat. Add the Porkette and the scallions and cook until the Porkette starts to brown. Add 2 cups of fried potatoes and cilantro, then ¼ cup sauce and stir until the potatoes are just coated. Taste and add a tablespoon more if you like; reserve remaining sauce for another use. Serve with fried eggs."}], "nutrition": {"calories": 1487.9, "protein_g": 11.3, "carbohydrates_g": 115.6, "fat_g": 113.4, "saturated_fat_g": 24.2, "trans_fat_g": 0.0, "cholesterol_mg": 30.5, "sodium_mg": 1686.5, "fiber_g": 12.0, "sugar_g": 32.8, "unsaturated_fat_g": 83.6, "source": "nyt", "basis": "per_ser